A||||15||curtis||flowmaster exhaust||||5||
Z||000000||curtis||03-22-2000||11:11 PM||||does anyone know the model or the part number<BR>for a Flow Master muffler that will work on my<BR>'88 GT ? <BR>Modifications needed to make ?<BR> <BR>I was given to understand that there is a single<BR>inlet and dual outlet that is made to install for<BR>a Camero/ Firebird that will work in a Fiero.<BR> <BR>I am trying to get better flow and alittle more noise<BR>to come out. Any suggestions could really help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||TalShino||03-22-2000||11:35 PM||||Anyone got any suggestions on a muffler to make my 88 coupe 2.5liter near silent.. heh.. or atleast very quiet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Ray -TalShino||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||03-23-2000||12:41 AM||||Certainly not a Flowmaster. I had one on the Ciera GT and it lasted 5 minutes before I took it off. Mother's were pulling their children indoors and several small animals were killed.<P>I have a Dynomax now. Nice and subtle.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-23-2000||08:28 AM||||Flowmaster Delta-Flow 50 series. Nice and deep but opens up nice when your hard in the gas.. Have one on my 86 V6. All 2.5" pipe witn 2.5" Cat. As for the silent.. uum Bose and Siemens have teamed up to make an affordable anti-noise muffler/filter. for about $1700 it will make no noise except wind noise... On a more reasonable note.. on a 4 banger a stock system and add a 16" Blue cherry bomb right down from the cat and that will help a bit... act as another muffler,  Why ya looking for quiet? the factory unit works pretty well!?||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||03-23-2000||12:08 PM||||The flowmaster part number is: 42583.<BR>You may also want to consider the Power-Flo #30079 from SLP.  They are "manufactured entirely of 304 stainless steel" and only cost $210.<P>Street Legal Performance (SLP):<BR> <A HREF="http://www.slpeng.com/slpperformanceparts/page_16.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.slpeng.com/slpperformanceparts/page_16.html</A> <P>Mike<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||03-23-2000||12:56 PM||||After I put in my tri-y header on my 87 4 cyl I very quickly blew out the muffler that had done its job so well for so long. I had dynomax installed and was very pleased. It was very quiet but after I took off the cat it was the perfect noise I wanted.<P>Flowmasters are for those who want no children on the road (after the mothers pull them in) and can use it instead of the horn wake up the lowered Honda asleep at the light. With my 85 GT I am going with a cat and no muffler with 2.5 in exhaust. The exhaust tips will be 4.5 inches across (one on either side) and actually have the function of quieting some of the blast coming through. (in theory). Cant wait to let you guys know the results!||||reg||14
Z||000006||Bruce||03-23-2000||10:01 PM||||The neverending muffler saga.........<BR>I bought a flowmaster last summer with ever intention of having it installed, but I know the installation is a major ($) job, and the increased noise doesn't appeal to me. I just want maybe one notch above the stock sound. I know the Walker makes a muffler specifically for the Fiero, but I don't have any reports about installation and sound.<BR>So, opinions please.<BR>thanks,<BR>bb||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bruce||03-23-2000||10:02 PM||||Incidentally, I'm thinking of selling the Flowmaster. Any offers?||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FieroGTRacingInc||03-24-2000||07:37 AM||||HHmmm....  I'll Bite...  *crunch*  How much Bruce???||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bruce||03-24-2000||06:17 PM||||I bought it for $72, as I recall, and it's still in the box. Have to figure in shipping (it's a heavy mama). What do you think is a fair price?<BR>bb||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierosound||03-25-2000||02:02 PM||||$210 for a SLP muffler???  And some people thought $420 for a Borla exhaust system was pricey!!!||||reg||10
Z||000011||lowCG||03-25-2000||04:56 PM||||At least an SLP unit is 2-1/2",and will flow better,with adequate noise(rapp) control,guaranteed for life.I've used this type of muffler before,and even though the design is very simple,they work very well.Seems alot for what it is though,or maybe I don't know much about quality muffler construction.The Flowmaster mufflers do an ok job considering they have no packing,however they kind of sound like there's an old coffee can wired over an open collector when used on a healthy motor.||||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||03-26-2000||11:37 AM||||My motor must not be all that ballsy then. Either that or I have a different model Flowmaster the other forum members have experienced. My car puts out a nastier tone then stock, to be sure, but is far from sounding like an "empty coffe can".  Could it be that folks are mounting "off road" versions of the Flowmaster?   ||||reg||1
Z||000013||87PegasusGT||03-27-2000||04:59 PM||||If I go buy this Delta Force 50 muffler whatsit, am I going to have to modify it to fit?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Bruce||03-27-2000||08:42 PM||||The stock muffler case alone is 11 1/2" long, <BR>10" high, and 3 1/2" wide, approximately, and that excludes the inlet/outlet tubing. It's a tight fit at that, so you'ld better check the delta flow dimensions, so you'll have a basic idea of what you're up against for installation.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||03-28-2000||12:33 AM||||Looking at the lack of space for a muffler,I now realize that the SLP unit won't exacly fit that great,because of the shape of the frame(they made a dippy-do around the stock muffler,how nice).<BR>So,it's either a Flowmaster,or make my own unit with the inlet pipe facing the motor,and all the bends inside the can,and the outlets facing the rear of the car,using perforated tubing,and stainless wool/curlies,what alot of work,but it might be better than some raspy,crackly setup.<BR>||||reg||1
